# Term representation and a
# directed, terminating simplification core -- pure forward-chaining
# rules, no C++ involvement.
#
# PREREQUISITE: import one of the arithmetic modules first (arithmetic,
# binary-arithmetic or binary-nand-arithmetic); the &-literals below are
# parsed via zelph/number at import time. The module is representation-
# agnostic: &0/&1 resolve to whatever the loaded module builds, so
# symbolic results share their numeral nodes with numeric computation.

# Default arithmetic substrate; import a different provider of
# "arithmetic" (or claim the ID via .provides) BEFORE this module to override:
.import binary-arithmetic

# TERMS are ordinary graph structure:
#   - symbolic atoms: named nodes with declared sorts
#       x ~ symvar        (variables)
#       c ~ symconst      (opaque base constants)
#   - binary operators: the SAME predicates as those in numeric
#     modules (+, *, /) -- a symbolic (x + y) and a numeric (&2 + &3) both
#     encode knowledge about the same + node
#   - unary application: (F of U) where the function symbol F functions as
#     a first-class node; properties of function symbols are ordinary facts
#     (inverseof below), processed by a single generic inverse-pair rule --
#     any undeclared function symbol operates seamlessly by default and
#     simply lacks rewrites
#   - numerals: cons lists originating from the arithmetic module, leaves
#     here that simplify to their canonical form per the SB rules
#
# GATING: nothing fires spontaneously. Work is seeded by the request
# self-fact (T simplify T) -- the testprime idiom -- and recursion is
# confined to marked terms (T needssimp T), following the needscanon
# pattern. Numeric facts remain untouched. (Conversely, the numeric
# triggers DO fire on symbolic +, *, / facts and create internal states
# such as ((x add y) ci 0). These are harmless -- whatever they derive
# is true, and a symbolic operand never leads to an = result -- and
# remain finite per fact. On atoms, digit recursion stalls immediately:
# one state for +, -, and *, and for / one seed per digit, keyed by the
# divisor, plus a dvd state. A numeral operand is traversed, however:
# the left factor of *, the dividend of /, and the trial products of a
# numeral divisor, which run to completion. Their count increases with
# the numeral's length, and the completed multiples table is the one
# later numeric division by that numeral reuses. They are the price of
# the shared predicates that make constant folding (M3) possible.)
#
# NORMAL-FORM CONTRACT for authors of rewrites, in two parts.
# (1) Each rw right-hand-side INSTANCE must itself be in normal form. A
# rule meets this when at most the root of its instance is newly
# introduced: it constitutes a subterm within the reduced form; or it is
# a canonical leaf (a declared atom, a canonical numeral, a negative
# zint) -- a non-canonical numeral like <00> does not qualify as its own
# normal form; or it is a single new node built upon such parts to which
# no rule applies. SX and ZX fall into the final category, and their
# guards are specifically intended for precisely this purpose --
# although a declaration concerning that new node causes a rule to apply
# to it after all (refer to SX). This requirement is the reason a single
# bottom-up pass is adequate.
# (2) simp is single-valued due to an additional constraint: the rule
# set is conflict-free -- rules that match a given reduced form yield
# the same node. This is argued beside SR, SX, and SN below, and rule SU
# reports any violation.
# All rules listed below satisfy both parts. Rules that breach the
# contract (for instance, distributivity) need an iterated design and
# must NOT be added here without careful consideration.
#
# NEGATION: the single deferred rule (identity fallback) negates rw. Its
# consequences can, through congruence, grow rw for OTHER terms -- yet
# solely within the positive stratum, and the fallback for a term T
# becomes evaluable only once (T red C) is established, which arises
# during the same positive saturation that also derives T's rw (if any)
# -- AS LONG AS every negated rule that rw depends on has already
# finished. The NAND substrate's gate completion is one: its digit tables
# serve as the foundation for computing (&2 + &3) = &5. The engine's
# negation levels run such a rule to full completion before triggering
# the fallback (see reasoning_strata.cpp); up to version 1.0.1, both
# processes occurred in a single pass, and a request evaluated alongside
# the NAND bootstrap answered both itself and &5. The alternating
# stratified schedule therefore evaluates each negation against the fully
# realized relevant state; test_symbolic.cpp verifies this through chains
# that force multiple stratum alternations, whereas test_stratified.cpp
# achieves this via the NAND bootstrap. The levels remain valid as long
# as no rule within the same evaluation is considered to produce every
# predicate: a rule that takes the predicate it creates from an argument,
# like an inverse or sub-property rule concerning predicate variables,
# recombines the gate rule and the fallback into a single level. A
# transitivity or symmetry rule involving a predicate variable does not,
# nor does the inverse rule written as a rule that derives rules
# (logic.md, "Rules over predicates").
#
# DOMAIN CAVEAT: identities are FORMAL. exp inverseof ln (exp(ln u) = u)
# assumes u > 0 over the reals; &0 / X ignores division by zero. Side
# conditions are not tracked yet -- declare only directions you accept.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Function-symbol properties: F inverseof G means F(G(u)) = u
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
exp inverseof ln
ln inverseof exp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule ST0 -- Trigger: (T simplify T) is the user-facing request
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T simplify T) => (T needssimp T)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SD -- Decompose: mark all subterms (closed operator set)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) needssimp (U + V)) => (U needssimp U)
((U + V) needssimp (U + V)) => (V needssimp V)
((U * V) needssimp (U * V)) => (U needssimp U)
((U * V) needssimp (U * V)) => (V needssimp V)
((U / V) needssimp (U / V)) => (U needssimp U)
((U / V) needssimp (U / V)) => (V needssimp V)
((F of U) needssimp (F of U)) => (U needssimp U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SB -- Base cases: a declared atom is its own normal form, a
# numeral simplifies to its canonical form, and every other list is its
# own normal form. Undeclared atoms get no simp at all -- partiality by
# absence: declare your sorts, or the request stays silently unanswered
# (never wrongly answered).
#
# A cons list constitutes a leaf whose reduced form is (L red L). A
# numeral rewrites to its canonical form, thus <01> simplifies to &1;
# every other list undergoes no rewrite and remains its own normal form
# via the identity fallback (rules SS). When treated as its own normal
# form, <01> met rules that match &1 by node and an SN fold returning
# canonical numerals: (<01> * &1) simplified to both &1 and <01>, and
# rule SU reported the contradiction. The numeral test comes first
# because canon strips trailing zero cells from any list, transforming
# <x 0> into <x>. The cost: each numeral leaf passes the numeral test
# and canon before obtaining its simp, and a list that is not a numeral
# gets its simp solely within the negation stratum.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(X needssimp X, X ~ symvar) => (X simp X)
(X needssimp X, X ~ symconst) => (X simp X)
((A cons R) needssimp (A cons R)) => ((A cons R) red (A cons R))
((A cons R) needssimp (A cons R)) => ((A cons R) needsnumeral (A cons R))
((A cons R) needssimp (A cons R), (A cons R) isnumeral true)
=> ((A cons R) needscanon (A cons R))
((A cons R) needssimp (A cons R), (A cons R) isnumeral true, (A cons R) canonnum N)
=> ((A cons R) rw N)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SC -- Congruence: once the children are normal, build the
# context-reduced form. fact() is hash-consed, so a term whose children
# were already normal reduces to ITSELF (T red T) -- a self-fact.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
((U + V) needssimp (U + V), U simp P, V simp Q) => ((U + V) red (P + Q))
((U * V) needssimp (U * V), U simp P, V simp Q) => ((U * V) red (P * Q))
((U / V) needssimp (U / V), U simp P, V simp Q) => ((U / V) red (P / Q))
((F of U) needssimp (F of U), U simp P) => ((F of U) red (F of P))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SR -- Rewrites at the top level applied to reduced forms.
# Commutativity is deliberately excluded as a rule (rules solely
# introduce facts, never delete them; free commutativity would duplicate
# each term) -- both directions are specified individually instead. The
# rule set is conflict-free: if two rules match a single reduced form,
# they yield the same result node.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red (X + &0)) => (T rw X)
(T red (&0 + X)) => (T rw X)
(T red (X * &1)) => (T rw X)
(T red (&1 * X)) => (T rw X)
(T red (X * &0)) => (T rw &0)
(T red (&0 * X)) => (T rw &0)
(T red (X / &1)) => (T rw X)
(T red (&0 / X)) => (T rw &0)

# ln 1 = 0 -- exact, no domain caveat. Conflict-free with the inverse-pair
# rule below: &1 is a numeral, never an (exp of U) application.
(T red (ln of &1)) => (T rw &0)

# `exp 0 = 1` -- the companion of `ln 1 = 0`, and essential to it:
# lacking this, `exp(ln 1)` collapses bottom-up into `(exp of &0)` (since
# `ln 1` fires before the inverse-pair rule can recognize the `ln`), a
# case that versions up to 1.0.1 answered with &1.
(T red (exp of &0)) => (T rw &1)

# Generic inverse-pair rule -- one rule for every declared pair. Since
# U is a subterm of the reduced form, it is already normal; thus, the
# right-hand side fulfils the normal-form contract.
(F inverseof G, T red (F of (G of U))) => (T rw U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SX -- Constant reassociation: (m * (n * u)) folds m and n together.
#
# The sole associativity within this module, and by design: universal
# associativity combined with congruence causes the term space to expand
# uncontrollably, yet this particular instance reduces the measure
# (transforming two products into one), and its right-hand side introduces
# a new node to which no rule applies, thus a single bottom-up pass
# remains sufficient.
#
# One exception: a = fact DECLARED regarding (K * U) gives knowledge
# folding (rule SN) a rewrite for it. A request directed at (K * U) itself
# then answers the declared value, whereas a term that this rule rewrites
# into (K * U) keeps (K * U) as its answer -- simplifying that answer once
# more alters it, and rule SU detects nothing, because each term continues
# to have one simp. The right-hand side constitutes a normal form solely
# when nothing is declared about it.
#
# It matters because iterated differentiation produces exactly this shape.
# d3(x^5)/dx3 assembles (&5 * (&4 * (&3 * x ^ &2))) -- every factor correct,
# yet unreadable as a third derivative. Bottom-up, these rules turn the
# inner pair into &12 and then the outer one into &60.
#
# The mirrored shape ((m * n) * u) needs no rule: congruence reduces the
# inner numeral product through the SN fold before the outer form is built.
#
# NUMERALS BY PATTERN, not by guard: matching (A cons R) restricts both
# factors to cons-list numerals of the loaded substrate, so no spurious
# cmp or * facts are created for symbolic operands -- and zint terms, which
# are not cons lists, are left to symbolic-integers.zph's own rule.
#
# Conflict-freedom with the SR block above, case by case: (X * &1) and
# (X * &0) cannot match, since the right operand here is a product node;
# (&1 * X) overlaps harmlessly, because K = &1 * n = n makes both paths
# yield the same node; and (&0 * X) is excluded by the K != &0 guard. The
# SN fold stays silent, a product with a non-numeral operand having no =
# fact.
#
# The guard sits on the RESULT, not on a factor. K is &0 exactly when a
# factor is -- which is the case the absorbing rules above already answer,
# and answer differently ((&0 * u) is not the normal form &0). Guarding the
# left factor with ((A cons R) != &0) would work too, but the result guard
# covers both factors at once and needs no second condition.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red ((A cons R) * ((B cons S) * U)))
=> ((A cons R) * (B cons S))

(T red ((A cons R) * ((B cons S) * U)),
 ((A cons R) * (B cons S)) = K, K != &0)
=> (T rw (K * U))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule SN -- Knowledge folding (M3). When the graph knows an equational
# result for the reduced form, adopt it as the rewrite. For numeral
# operands, this constitutes constant folding without specialized
# infrastructure: the red fact IS an ordinary +/*// fact, prompting the
# arithmetic module's triggers to fire and derive (C = R) within the same
# positive stratum -- fully before the deferred identity fallback is
# evaluated. The rule is intentionally broader: ANY = fact concerning the
# reduced form is consumed, computed or declared -- yet ONLY = facts
# regarding the reduced form, never those pertaining to T as originally
# written. A declaration like ((x + &0) * &2) = c is never read, and no
# report is issued: the request reduces that term to (x * &2) before this
# rule engages. The rationale is that the term as written includes the
# arithmetic's computed = fact as well, and for operands starting with
# zero, that result is not canonical: (<01> + <010>) = <011>. Reading it
# would impose a second simp on the term. Declared equations must comply
# with the normal-form contract on their right-hand side. A declared
# equation involving the reduced form that conflicts with its rewrite, or
# that arrives after the request has been resolved, introduces a second
# simp to the term -- rule SU below identifies this as a contradiction,
# explicitly naming both.
#
# Confluence with the SR rules: at every overlap (for instance, red (&2 +
# &0), matched by SR and computed numerically), both routes produce the
# SAME node, since numeric results of canonical operands are canonical --
# addition by construction; subtraction, multiplication, and division
# through canonnum. The simplified operands inside a reduced form are
# simp results, the exponent in ^ among them, and SB rules guarantee that
# every numeral within is canonical.
# Partiality composes: (&5 / &0) lacks a = fact, lacks a rw, and falls
# back to itself as its own normal form.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red C, C = R) => (T rw R)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SS -- Connect simp: the rewrite result if one exists, otherwise
# the reduced form itself. The fallback is the module's only negation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T red C, T rw S) => (T simp S)
(T red C, ¬(T rw S)) => (T simp C)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules SE -- Definitional extension: an operator defined through its
# DEFINITION T = R rather than through rewrites. Its congruence rule
# builds (T expandsto R), where R is formed from the simp outcomes of
# the operands; R is subsequently simplified as an ordinary term, and
# its normal form becomes that of T.
#
# A defined term is deliberately free of any red fact, thereby
# preventing the identity fallback referenced earlier from being
# triggered: T's sole simp is R's, and R's is single-valued just as any
# term's is (refer to the contract above and rule SU). (An alternative
# method -- a red form that rw's to R's simp -- would let T's own
# fallback activate during the same deferred pass in which R first
# acquires a simp, before T's rw is established: leading to two
# answers.)
#
# CONTRACT for definitional operators: R must not contain the defined
# operator (as R is processed through an ordinary single pass, any
# defined operator inside it would fail to have a standalone normal
# form), and R is built from the operands' simp results, thus
# re-marking them re-derives the same normal forms. Consequently,
# normal forms are free of defined operators. Cost: R, and each node
# within it located above the operands' normal forms, is newly created
# per T; the normal forms are shared.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T expandsto R) => (R needssimp R)
(T expandsto R, R simp S) => (T simp S)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule SU -- simp is single-valued. The full framework is designed to
# ensure this characteristic: a single bottom-up pass, rewrites using
# normal forms on the right-hand side, and a rule set free of conflicts.
# Anything the rules cannot exclude originates with the user: a declared
# equation involving a reduced form that disagrees with its rewrite, or
# one that arrives after the corresponding request has already been
# resolved through the fallback mechanism (the engine never retracts its
# original response). In both cases, a term ends up with a second simp,
# and this rule reports such a situation as a contradiction by explicitly
# naming both, in every evaluation mode.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T simp A, T simp B, A != B) => !

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule SQ0 -- Connect: expose the result under =, testprime-style.
# Repeatable query idiom: (T simplify T) = X
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(T simplify T, T simp S) => ((T simplify T) = S)
